Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 19:14:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 15/16] feat(web): drive redirection through a JavaScript file
 system

The web client could not share drives: it has no file system of its own.
A host that has one (an Electron application, say) can now hand in a
JavaScript object with synchronous file operations through the
`drive_redirection` extension, with the drives to share, and the new
`WasmDriveBackend` serves the server's drive I/O requests (MS-RDPEFS
2.2.3.3) with it: create/open with the create dispositions, read, write,
query and set information, query volume information, directory
enumeration with DOS patterns, delete on close, rename, lock control
(granted), flush. Paths reach the object as the server sends them,
relative to the drive; the object resolves them inside the folder it
shares. Errors it throws are mapped to NT statuses by their Node-style
`code`.

The backend answers each request in place, as the Unix backend in
ironrdp-rdpdr-native does; the JavaScript object is wrapped in a `Send`
newtype, sound on the single WebAssembly thread. With drives, the
virtual printer is left out of the session (one RDPDR backend per
connection).

`driveRedirection(fs)` in iron-remote-desktop-rdp builds the extension
and documents the `DriveFs` interface.
